{
  "requirement": "Plain text defines no attachments. The file root and each `line` item have\nempty attachment lists, including lines that begin with comment delimiters\nfrom another language.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has one obligation. Plain text defines no attachments, so the file root and each `line` item have empty attachment lists. That holds even for lines that begin with comment delimiters from another language.\n\nThe marked test `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs#fn[bundled_languages_classify_only_their_attachment_syntax]` covers it. Its `test.txt` case uses the source `# just text\\n`. That is a line starting with a comment delimiter from another language (the one Python, YAML and TOML use). The expected attachment list is `vec![]`.\n\nThe assertion flat-maps `attachments` over every item in `snapshot.outline().items`. `Outline.items` is the whole preorder item list. The helper `fn[plain_line_fingerprints_use_sha256_words_without_losing_boundaries]/fn[line]` in the same file treats `items[1]` as the first line item, so the list includes the root at index 0 and the line items after it. The comparison is an `assert_eq!` against the empty vector, so a spurious `Comment` or `Annotation` on the root or on the line would fail the test.\n\nOnly `#` is tried as a foreign delimiter. Other delimiters such as `//`, `/*` or `<!--` belong to the same class, so I don't count them as a separate required scenario. The test carries the `{test}` reference to the subject, so there is no marking gap.",
  "references": "1ef306a66d16e7b2dae06e3da1cb46df121252836c2196dd6eda170dff068010",
